{{smaller block|His eyes would have ''watered'' with a true feeling over the sale of a widow’s furniture.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, i. 12.}}}}
'''2.''' To gather saliva as a symptom of appetite: said of the mouth or teeth, and in figurative use noting vehement desire or craving.
{{smaller block|In theyr mindes they conceaued a hope of a daintie banquet, And, espying their enemies a farre of, beganne to swalowe theyr spettle as their mouthes ''watered'' for greedines of theyr pray.
{{right|''Peter Martyr'' (tr. in Eden’s First Books on America, ed. [Arber, p. 181).}}}}
{{smaller block|Oh, my little green gooseberry, my teeth ''waters'' at ye!
{{right|''Farquhar'', Love and a Bottle, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|The dog’s mouth ''waters'' only at the sight of food, but the gourmand’s mouth will also ''water'' at the thought of it.
{{right|''J. Ward'', Encyc. Brit., XX. 57.}}}}
'''3.''' To get or take in water: as, the ship put into port to ''water''; specifically, to drink water.
{{smaller block|We ''watered'' at the Canaries, we traded with the Salvages at Dominica.
{{right|Quoted in ''Capt. John Smith’s'' Works, I. 150.}}}}
{{smaller block|Were I a poet, by Hippocrene I swear (which was a certain well where all the Muses ''watered''), etc.
{{right|''Dekker and Webster'', Westward Ho, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|A Mischance befel the Horse, which lamed him as he went a ''watering'' to the Seine.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, I. i. 17.}}}}

